#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475988} :activity/id #uuid "8ffeb28b-7042-5986-93be-16c63236c88f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048449 :topic/id #uuid "a05758d1-f2b4-5a86-b7b0-c2a4bb6dcb85" :topic/math-academy-id 1931} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475988 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d118fc5-1c52-5566-be98-a9707e774937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377697} {:answer/id #uuid "30aa323e-111a-55db-ae16-f96b2c70da3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377698} {:answer/id #uuid "563f0bf5-f3be-54dc-b44e-fce9a03892d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377699} {:answer/id #uuid "a81b9d36-0a30-5151-bf36-ec51a5b12490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377700} {:answer/id #uuid "f84b3786-3b86-57a7-acce-0bc703bbfb0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377701}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377699} :answer-field/id #uuid "117ca5de-bfde-5504-8e9b-162dc1ee9296" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d22711b-d1d6-5f06-ab4a-74275f71d77c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74920" :question/problem "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)-(-3)}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{(x-1)y}{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-3)}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-3)}y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-3.$"} :step/id #uuid "13485a67-00ba-5d4e-a4ce-60195066ea29" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475990}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475990 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a374dad-4f6b-524a-aa54-86b138bf78f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342993} {:answer/id #uuid "63a029bd-31fb-55ab-850e-9758cb89df9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343081} {:answer/id #uuid "8849f804-0c8c-53f5-8a8d-500546d52a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343419} {:answer/id #uuid "8a9051dc-e91b-5b6b-8909-a2310cbbc28e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343442} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9e25dd-2c46-5b76-99a3-260d2e1e558e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343907}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343081} :answer-field/id #uuid "b313de74-d195-5d45-bc88-f2346a3b2f91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "774103b5-b3b5-52ef-9d71-0bba2999450c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74957" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{1+2}}{3-1-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{y+2})^{2}}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{x-y-2}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{1+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}.$"} :step/id #uuid "3925855e-b8a5-539c-a189-1799c0ceb8ac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475997}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074a6788-72f7-50c1-a035-0612abea7cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380244} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b6b773-681c-58f9-b0ee-b6ee7442117a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380245} {:answer/id #uuid "94a9a1ac-d347-5b5f-9ae4-3572ba829da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380246} {:answer/id #uuid "91b1447d-4781-51f6-b677-110d8689bdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380247} {:answer/id #uuid "8852c79a-f54e-5a8f-87c0-2803442d73d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380247} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f7b683-d099-548b-9b98-dddfeba7f07a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fb25e06c-c7b0-5dbe-83ae-8f7b0c5dbf13" :question/math-academy-id "q-86868" :question/problem "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)+1}{(-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+x+y}{xy+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x(x+y)+(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+1)(x+y)}{y(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+1}{y} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"} :step/id #uuid "5c7e6dac-de5a-50c9-a4a3-207c8cf4283a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475997 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5225a54e-55a7-5758-ada6-d6e76ec121f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342920} {:answer/id #uuid "6afe5058-ed29-5490-84ca-cf5ea037f221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343150} {:answer/id #uuid "72810408-6a46-5e8d-9beb-52bbe2e8f436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343231} {:answer/id #uuid "c186d6e7-0ac4-5259-897c-07e60f6cc561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343939} {:answer/id #uuid "fd70e6c7-52f3-50c1-9a16-a6b4b41da448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343150} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5121edc-c04d-5ea2-9e7a-1a1ca983765e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "580ed3de-301a-5585-aa08-af0b5b637cec" :question/math-academy-id "q-74960" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=\\frac{{1}^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{-(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{x-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{1}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "a1d76783-98ca-587b-9e64-3ae5ae7e97c5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475994}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1771b53b-a9ea-5204-9e6c-c3664e7f4eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475475} {:answer/id #uuid "52b82999-7d0c-5f60-b09d-5d9bfb9dabdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475563} {:answer/id #uuid "644db317-5ce2-5b55-a3a9-b57771242cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475590} {:answer/id #uuid "9e112b0b-f642-5793-ba13-d921da5ca298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475677} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d267a1-bd0b-57f4-92ed-545ceb83bbea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475475} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe5df83-a1f1-5212-94d6-8a03a2936996" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "83c8f2ad-db17-53f8-868d-e48deabc8feb" :question/math-academy-id "q-129409" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,2)}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}\n$$\n\nI. The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=2$\n\nII. The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=x+1$\n\nIII. The limit does not exist" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2)}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}} & =\\frac{(1-1)(2-2)}{(1-1)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0(0)}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(1,2)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (1,2)$ along the path $y=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2)\\,\\,\\,y=2}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(2-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(0)}{(x-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (1,2)$ along the path $y=x+1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2)\\,\\,\\,y=x+1}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(x+1-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(x+1-2)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)(x-1)}{(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2(x-1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)^{2}}}{2\\cancel{(x-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,2)}\\,\\frac{(x-1)(y-2)}{(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d56691a3-bd81-5d30-bd36-57d7d11c4bf9"}] :activity/title "Limits and Continuity of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475440}]}
